WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.509
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:10.336
We will now talk about how this issue of
zeros in

00:00:10.336 --> 00:00:15.990
randomized algorithms can be addressed. And
it's not a minor annoyance,

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:19.990
it's actually a serious issue because
randomized algorithms do

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:23.950
do not like working with
zeroes.

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:29.018
We will now learn how to deal with zeroes
in the profile matrix.

00:00:29.018 --> 00:00:32.260
And in the real world, there is always a
possibility

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:36.180
that we do not observe one of the possible
events.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.580
In this case, we assign them the empirical
probability zero.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:43.070
But in fact, their real probability is not
zero.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:46.330
It's simply the size of our sample is too
small.

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:50.460
And randomized algorithm hate zeroes
because zeroes

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.560
affect their performance in a serious
way.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.520
That's why we need to learn how to get rid
of zeroes.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:02.980
Fortunately, Laplace helped us to figure out
how to do this.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:08.400
Laplace calculated the probability that
the sun will not rise tomorrow, 

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.400
given the fact that it has risen in the
last 5,000 years.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:18.239
And this probability is roughly
one in two million, according to Laplace.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:22.780
His estimate was ridiculed because his
opponents

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:26.030
did not realize how important this
calculation was.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.050
If we repeat an experiment that
can result in a

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:37.910
success or a failure n times and get s
successes,

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:42.720
then what is the probability that the next
repetition will be a success?

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.270
In other words, if X_1, X_2,

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:52.482
X_{n+1} conditionally independent Boolean

00:01:52.482 --> 00:01:58.050
random variables (where failure corresponds
to 0 and success corresponds to 1), 

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:04.950
then the traditional way to compute
the probability of X_{n+1} is simply

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:11.850
to compute the fraction of successes among
among all trials, so it will be s/n.

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:17.860
However, since we have a prior knowledge,

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:23.820
that successes and failures exist, then
they actually have

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:29.370
two more observations implicitly, even
before we started our trials.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:31.970
So, even before we started our trials we

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:36.860
should acknowledge that success and
failure are possible and

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:40.240
therefore there are two more "invisible"
events.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:46.090
That was Laplace argument, and therefore
he made a correction

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:50.790
based on the fact that when we see n
events, we actually see n+2 events.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:55.020
And when we see s successes, we actually
see s+1 successes, 

00:02:55.020 --> 00:03:02.000
and the formula changes to (s+1)/(n+1).

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.740
It may sound counter-intuitive but let's
see how Laplace

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:09.850
would get rid of zeroes in the profile
matrix.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:14.900
In this case, what we need to do

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.570
to update the profile matrix to get rid of
zero?

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:22.465
According to the Laplace's rule of
succession, we

00:03:22.465 --> 00:03:27.215
should assume a possibility that every
event represented in the

00:03:27.215 --> 00:03:30.820
profile matrix can argue.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:35.920
And therefore we should add 1s to every
entry of the profile matrix.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.808
And here I show how Laplace would update
the

00:03:39.808 --> 00:03:43.900
the count matrix by adding 1s
to all entries.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:48.750
As a result, the profile matrix also will get
updated, all probabilities

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:52.880
will get updated, and we 

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:54.230
will get rid of all zeroes.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:04:00.050
Now, instead of a rolling a one-sided die, we
can actually roll a seven-sided die.

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:04.520
And based on the result of rolling a seven-sided
die, we will

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:08.940
choose a new instance of the motif in the
sequence.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:10.430
Here it is.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:15.470
Afterwards, we iterate, and let's see how
our motifs are changing.

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:18.640
Starting from this, we once again
continue

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:22.760
with Gibbs sampler, 
remove one of the sequences, 

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:30.250
form motif matrix, build count matrix, 
it

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:33.780
update it with pseudocounts by adding ones to every
entry, 

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:39.330
compute profile matrix once again, compute
probability of every k-mer, 

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:44.904
and once again roll a seven sided die.
The seven-sided

00:04:44.904 --> 00:04:50.090
die leads to changing once again one of
the choices of our motif and we

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:52.340
start a new iteration.

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:58.530
Again, we randomly remove one of the
sequences, construct count matrix, 

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:03.750
apply Laplace's rule of succession, 
create profile, 

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:08.730
calculate probability again,
roll a seven-sided die,                              

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:14.070
and derive (once again,
starting from completely random motif), 

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:16.080
in just three iterations,

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:19.870
we almost captured the correct
implanted motifs.

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:26.100
Which means that the bias that implanted
motif introduced was correctly

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.520
captured by Gibbs Sampler, and led us to
the correct motif.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.910
We are not still there, because, in the
second

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:35.270
sequence, we have not found the correct
motif yet.

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:40.330
But surely, at the next iteration, with
high probability, we'll find it.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:41.590
We're almost

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:45.910
done with covering randomized algorithms,
but the

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:51.080
challenge of predicting the degenerative
signals remains.

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:55.760
Remember how randomized motifs search did
not find

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:00.340
the perfect binding sites for
this motif?

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:01.040
What can we do?

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:06.690
Well, there is one possibility.
Maybe, maybe we can develop even better

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:08.760
algorithm for finding motifs, but if you

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.380
think about this, imagine that you implant 
not

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:16.230
not a motif motif of length 15 with 4
mutations,

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:20.320
but motif of length 15 with 5 
mutations.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:24.080
Finding this motif is almost
impossible, not because we don't have

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:28.110
efficient algorithms, but because random
motifs

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:32.490
start competing with real motifs. Simply
statistically,

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:38.200
it may not be possible to
find this motif if we

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:44.585
implant them in long DNA strings.
For example into strings of length 1,000.

00:06:44.585 --> 00:06:45.830
Well, what can be done?

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:51.570
Should we give up in this case?
Biologist did not give up and the

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:57.560
way to solve this problem today is
to resort to the technique

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:01.370
which is called ChIP-sequencing.
Essentially what the cheap ChiP-sequencing

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:07.760
does, it reduces the length of DNA strings
to look for regulatory motifs.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:12.420
As I mentioned, when you insert elusive
patterns in the

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:18.260
strings of length 1,000, it may not be possible
even to find it even with the best

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:23.280
available algorithms.
But if biologists reduced the area

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:28.420
where to look for motif, let's say
from 1,000 to 100, then suddenly

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:35.410
statistics works in our favor, and we can
find such motifs.

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:40.501
And this is the end of the story about
randomized algorithms.

